# The app's whole GraphQL surface, grouped by noun: shared vocabulary first, then
# Cluster, ClusterCache, cluster data, and the cloud account, with the root
# operation types collected at the end.
#
# --- Shared vocabulary ---
# The scalars and the kind-agnostic projections (Condition, Event, Schedule,
# DeltaFrameType) every noun's surface reuses. A type only one noun uses belongs in that
# noun's section, however generic its name reads.

"An ISO-8601 encoded UTC date string."
scalar Time

"""
The identity of a persisted object (a cluster, a cluster's cache, …), opaque to
clients (a decimal-string id on the wire). One scalar for every object kind so
the same id machinery is reused. Use values returned by the server verbatim; do
not construct or parse them.

Object ids are unique across kinds, not just within one, so they never collide —
which is why a lookup handed another kind's id answers null rather than erroring
or returning the wrong record. `Event.id` reuses this scalar for its wire form
only: an event run is not an object, its id comes from a separate sequence, and it
is unique within one timeline rather than across the fleet. Never hand one where
an object id is expected.
"""
scalar ObjectID

"""
The identity of a cluster record: its row id, opaque text a client uses verbatim.
Distinct from `ObjectID`, which names a cache or a per-kind record; a cluster is
never addressed by one.
"""
scalar ClusterID

"""
An arbitrary JSON value carried verbatim. Backs a cached Kubernetes object's full
native body: the sidecar forwards the stored, decompressed object JSON as-is (no
per-field GraphQL typing), and the frontend casts it to a typed Kubernetes object.
"""
scalar JSON

"A condition's three-valued verdict, Kubernetes-style."
enum ConditionStatus {
  "The condition holds."
  True
  "The condition does not hold."
  False
  "The condition cannot currently be assessed."
  Unknown
}

"""
One Kubernetes-style status condition: an independently-tracked aspect of the
record's observed state. Connection-controller conditions (on `Cluster.conditions`),
two aspects with two subjects — whether we reached it, and whether these credentials
could name it: `Connected` (the last probe reached the API server; reasons
Connecting/Connected/ResolveFailed/ProbeFailed/Inactive) and `Identified` (the probe
could tell which cluster answered; reasons Identified/UIDUnreadable/Connecting/
NoConnection/Inactive). Reaching a server needs no authorization and naming it does, so
`Identified` fails on its own — and a cluster it fails for never gets a `ClusterCache`,
which is named for the identity it mirrors. Each points at a different remedy: the
network or the kubeconfig for one, an RBAC grant for the other.
Sync-controller conditions: `Synced`, reported at two levels. On
`ClusterCache.conditions` it is coarse (reasons Syncing/Paused) — the cache states
only whether it decided to sync. The verdict a UI wants is the one on the cache's
sync children — the per-kind `ClusterCachedKind` records (reasons
Syncing/Watching/Paused/NoConnection/SyncFailed/Stale), or their whole-cache rollup
`clusterCacheHealthWatch`, which speaks the same vocabulary.
New types/reasons may appear; consumers should treat unknown spellings as a
degraded/unknown display state.
"""
type Condition {
  "The aspect this condition tracks, e.g. \"Connected\"."
  type: String!
  status: ConditionStatus!
  "CamelCase machine-readable explanation of `status`."
  reason: String!
  "Human-readable detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  """
  Whether this condition reports process-scoped state (a live connection, a running
  sync worker) rather than durable truth. A liveness condition written by a previous
  sidecar process reads as `Unknown` until the controller re-confirms it, so the UI
  never shows a stale `True` after a restart. `reason` is preserved across that
  downgrade, so it still says what the last known state was.
  """
  liveness: Boolean!
  """
  Whether `status` is a downgrade the sidecar derived rather than one a controller
  wrote: a liveness condition left behind by an earlier process, read as `Unknown`
  until re-confirmed. When true, `reason`, `message` and the stamps below describe
  the **last known** status, not this `Unknown` — so a consumer must not present
  them as current (no "Error" off a pre-restart reason, no "up for 0m").
  """
  unconfirmed: Boolean!
  "When `status` last changed — not when the condition was last refreshed. A downgraded liveness condition keeps the stamp of the write that set it, which predates the current process."
  transitionedAt: Time!
  "When the condition was last written, whether or not `status` changed."
  updatedAt: Time!
}

"""
A pointer to another record — today always the owner a child record hangs off, which
is the key the client folds its streams together on. Kind-agnostic, so the whole
ownership chain reads the same way rather than naming a differently-spelled id per
kind.
"""
type ObjectRef {
  "The referenced record's id."
  id: ObjectID!
  "Its kind, e.g. `\"Cluster\"` — for a consumer that has to tell owners apart."
  kind: String!
}

"An event's severity, mirroring the control plane's event type: Normal (✓) or Warning (✗)."
enum EventType {
  Normal
  Warning
}

"""
One coalesced run from an object's control-plane event timeline: consecutive
occurrences sharing an outcome (category, type, reason) are aggregated, so
`count`/`firstAt`/`lastAt` describe the run. A generic projection served under
every kind's events surface — `category` names the timeline (e.g. `"connection"`),
`reason` is an open, category-specific machine code; consumers should treat
unknown spellings as a degraded/unknown state. The client upserts a live stream
by `id` (a re-delivered id is an updated run; a new id is a new run).
"""
type Event {
  "Stable run identity — the client's upsert key on a watch. An opaque int64 id, same wire form as ObjectID, but from the event sequence: unique within this timeline, not across kinds."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The timeline this run belongs to, e.g. `\"connection\"`."
  category: String!
  type: EventType!
  "CamelCase machine code, e.g. `Connected` / `ProbeFailed`. Open-ended per category."
  reason: String!
  "The latest occurrence's detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  "Occurrences coalesced into this run (>= 1)."
  count: Int!
  "When the run started (first occurrence)."
  firstAt: Time!
  "When the run last extended (latest occurrence)."
  lastAt: Time!
}

"""
An object's reconcile-schedule gauge: when the control plane has queued the
object's next reconcile (a cluster's next backoff retry, for a disconnected one),
which a UI counts down to. Generic like Event — the watch entrypoints are
kind-scoped (clusterScheduleWatch) but the value shape is not. A struct (not a
bare Time) so it can grow. Observed live via a schedule watch — a scheduling
change fires no object/list watch, so this is the only way to keep the countdown
moving for an otherwise-idle object.
"""
type Schedule {
  "The scheduled time of the next reconcile, or null while a reconcile is in flight (see `probing`) or nothing is scheduled."
  nextRequeueAt: Time
  """
  Whether a connection probe is running right now. Asserted by the controller for
  the in-flight window (not inferred from a missing nextRequeueAt), so the UI can
  show a definite "checking now" while the probe's round-trips are in flight.
  """
  probing: Boolean!
}

"""
Classifies one frame on a delta watch, mirroring a Kubernetes watch event. On
subscribe a watch replays the current set as `Added` frames (the snapshot), closes it
with one `Bookmark`, then streams live changes. A `Deleted` carries the object's
last-known state; the client keys on its `id`.
"""
enum DeltaFrameType {
  Added
  Modified
  Deleted
  Bookmark
}

"""
Classifies one frame on an event-timeline watch. Two values where `DeltaFrameType` has
four: an event log is a positioned log, not a mirrored set. The server delivers the
snapshot plus what grows above it and never reports a prune, so a run is only ever
upserted and there is no `Deleted`.
"""
enum EventFrameType {
  "A run to upsert by `Event.id`."
  Run
  "Closes the on-subscribe snapshot; sent once, carrying no run."
  Bookmark
}

"""
One frame on an event-timeline watch (`eventsWatch`, whatever record's id it is given).

The accumulated set is append-only and can outgrow the server's: retention bounds
each timeline to a fixed number of runs, and a pruned run is not announced. A
long-lived consumer should cap what it keeps rather than expect the server to
evict for it.
"""
type EventWatchFrame {
  type: EventFrameType!
  "The run. Null on `Bookmark`."
  event: Event
}

# --- Cluster ---
# A tracked kube-context: its record, its connection observations, and its own
# event/schedule surfaces. The cache mirror beneath it is ClusterCache; the data
# synced into that cache is the cluster-data section.

"""
A registered cluster connection: a tracked kube-context paired with its on-disk
cache mirror. Modeled Kubernetes-style so a consumer knows where data comes
from: `spec` is the user-owned desired state, `status` the observed state. Two
records may point at the same physical cluster (`status.server.uid` tracks
physical identity). A cluster stays listed after its context leaves the
kubeconfig (so its cache can still be inspected or cleared).
"""
type Cluster {
  "Unique identifier for this cluster record."
  id: ClusterID!

  "When this cluster was first registered."
  createdAt: Time!
  "When the user last edited this cluster — a toggle, a rename, the delete. A probe never moves it."
  updatedAt: Time!
  "When this cluster's deletion was requested; null unless deletion is pending. The record lingers, marked, until its runtime and chats are torn down, and user-facing collections hide it meanwhile."
  deletionRequestedAt: Time

  "Desired state: the user-owned fields."
  spec: ClusterSpec!
  "Observed connection/health state, discovered by probing."
  status: ClusterStatus!
  "The connection controller's conditions (`Connected`, `Identified`)."
  conditions: [Condition!]!

  """
  Every cache this cluster has mirrored. Usually one; a UID migration leaves the
  superseded cache in place until its subtree drains, which is why this is a list.

  **None of them is marked active.** The active cache is the one whose `spec.serverUid`
  matches this cluster's `status.server.uid` — a comparison the caller makes, and
  must keep making: the parent's UID is rewritten by a probe, which fires no cache
  event, so an answer computed once goes stale silently. A consumer that needs to
  follow that over time reads `clustersWatch` and `clusterCachesWatch` and joins
  them live rather than reading this field.
  """
  caches: [ClusterCache!]!

  """
  This cluster's control-plane event timeline (newest run first), optionally
  filtered to one `category` (e.g. `"connection"`) and bounded by `limit`. Empty
  for a cluster with no events. Resolved on selection and decoupled from the
  cluster/list watch, so event chatter never re-emits the cluster — and a query
  that doesn't select it pays nothing.
  """
  events(category: String, limit: Int): [Event!]!
}

"A cluster record's desired state. Every field is user/API-owned; nothing here is observed."
type ClusterSpec {
  "User-assigned display name; null if unset."
  name: String
  "Whether the user has enabled this cluster in the app: it appears in cluster lists / context pickers and is eligible to connect. Disabled clusters stay tracked but dormant."
  enabled: Boolean!
  "Whether cache syncing is enabled. Disabling stops sync and freezes the cache. Only meaningful while enabled."
  syncEnabled: Boolean!
  "Whether the background monitor may schedule runs against this cluster. Off by default, since a run spends model calls; stored and served, and nothing runs on it yet."
  monitoringEnabled: Boolean!
  "Where this record comes from and how its credentials resolve."
  source: ClusterSpecSource!
}

"""
The discriminated union naming a cluster record's origin: exactly one variant
is set. Each variant is owned by exactly one creator (the kubeconfig source
today; manual creation and kstack-cloud sync are future variants).
"""
type ClusterSpecSource {
  "Set when the record tracks a kube-context in the user's kubeconfig."
  kubeconfig: ClusterSpecSourceKubeconfig
}

"The kubeconfig-sourced variant of ClusterSpecSource."
type ClusterSpecSourceKubeconfig {
  "The kube-context name this record tracks."
  context: String!
}

"""
A cluster record's observed connection/health state, persisted on the Cluster
record by the connection controller: the probed facts. The conditions that go with
them sit beside this block on `Cluster.conditions`, not inside it — the consumer
derives display state from their type/status/reason. Sync status and the on-disk
cache live on the ClusterCache children, not here.
"""
type ClusterStatus {
  "Per-source observation blocks, mirroring `spec.source`."
  source: ClusterStatusSource!
  "Last-known facts about the remote cluster (uid, version), discovered by probing."
  server: ClusterServer!
  "The connecting client's identity and effective access on this cluster."
  principal: ClusterPrincipal!
}

"""
The status-side counterpart of ClusterSpecSource: the per-source observation
blocks, of which exactly the spec source variant's is set. Null blocks mean
"this record's source has no such observation" — never that an observation
went away (an orphaned kubeconfig record keeps its block with
`isPresent: false`).
"""
type ClusterStatusSource {
  "Kubeconfig observation; null unless the record is kubeconfig-sourced."
  kubeconfig: ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfig
}

"""
A kubeconfig-sourced record's last-known kubeconfig observation: the cluster
and user names its context (`spec.source.kubeconfig.context`) references, and
whether the context is present. Cached from the last time the context was
present, so they survive the context leaving the kubeconfig and let the UI
identify an orphaned record. In a malformed kubeconfig the referenced entries
may not resolve.
"""
type ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfig {
  "The cluster half of the context: the entry name it references, and that entry; cached."
  cluster: ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigCluster!
  "The user half of the context; cached."
  user: ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigUser!
  "True while the context is currently present in the kubeconfig; false when orphaned."
  isPresent: Boolean!
  "True when the context is the kubeconfig's `current-context`; false otherwise."
  isDefault: Boolean!
}

"""
The cluster half of a context. `name` is stated by the context, `entry` by the file,
so the two go missing independently: a context always names a cluster, and the file
may define no entry under that name.
"""
type ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigCluster {
  "Name of the cluster entry the context references."
  name: String!
  "The entry `name` resolves to; null when the kubeconfig defines no such entry."
  entry: ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigClusterEntry
}

"""
The user (authInfo) half of a context. It carries the entry name alone: an authInfo
entry holds credentials, so anything served from it is a chosen projection, never a
mirror of the file.
"""
type ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigUser {
  "Name of the user entry the context references."
  name: String!
}

"""
A kubeconfig cluster entry. Fields are a subset of what the file holds, added as a
consumer needs them, and each is what the file says — no verdict is drawn here: a
client asking whether the connection is verified reads `insecureSkipTLSVerify` and
`server`'s scheme, both stated before anything is dialled.
"""
type ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfigClusterEntry {
  "The entry's `server` URL."
  server: String!
  "The entry's `insecure-skip-tls-verify`."
  insecureSkipTLSVerify: Boolean!
}

"Last-known facts about the remote cluster, discovered by probing. Fields are null until a successful probe."
type ClusterServer {
  "The cluster's `kube-system` namespace UID; stable across context renames."
  uid: String
  "Server version, e.g. \"v1.29.3\"."
  version: String
  "API server URL that answered; null until probed. Two contexts naming one server share it."
  endpoint: String
}

"The connecting client's identity and effective access on this cluster."
type ClusterPrincipal {
  "Authenticated username the API server last mapped this cluster's credentials to (SelfSubjectReview); null until probed."
  username: String
  "Groups the API server mapped these credentials into; empty until probed. Sorted. Where the principal's access usually comes from, since RBAC binds to groups more often than to users."
  groups: [String!]!
  "Effective RBAC in a namespace (live SelfSubjectRulesReview), resolved on demand. Point-in-time, for debugging only — not authoritative for access decisions."
  permissions(namespace: String!): ClusterPermissions!
}

"""
The connecting principal's effective RBAC in one namespace (live
SelfSubjectRulesReview). Namespace-scoped (the API has no all-namespaces form)
and point-in-time — for UI/debugging, not authoritative for access decisions.
"""
type ClusterPermissions {
  "The namespace these rules were evaluated for."
  namespace: String!
  "Allowed resource rules (verbs × apiGroups × resources)."
  resourceRules: [ResourceRule!]!
  "Allowed non-resource rules (verbs × URLs), e.g. /healthz."
  nonResourceRules: [NonResourceRule!]!
  "True if some authorizers couldn't be evaluated, so the rule set may be incomplete."
  incomplete: Boolean!
}

"A resource-scoped RBAC rule: the verbs the principal may use on the given apiGroups/resources (optionally narrowed to named instances)."
type ResourceRule {
  verbs: [String!]!
  apiGroups: [String!]!
  resources: [String!]!
  "Specific resource names this rule is restricted to; empty means all."
  resourceNames: [String!]!
}

"A non-resource RBAC rule: the verbs the principal may use against non-resource URLs (e.g. /healthz)."
type NonResourceRule {
  verbs: [String!]!
  nonResourceUrls: [String!]!
}

"One frame on the `clustersWatch` stream: a change and the cluster it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot."
type ClusterWatchFrame {
  "Whether this cluster was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected cluster. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; the client removes it by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  cluster: Cluster
}

# --- ClusterCache ---
# A cluster's on-disk mirror and the sync machinery under it: the cache record, the
# per-kind sync children, and the whole-cache verdict folded from them. The data these
# caches hold is the cluster-data section.

"""
A cache record, mirroring the beehive ClusterCache kind: a mirror of one physical
cluster identity. Streamed independently of its parent Cluster via
`clusterCachesWatch` and joined to it client-side by `clusterID`. Carries its
identity (`id`, `clusterID`, `spec.serverUid`) and its coarse `Synced` condition — there
is no status block, for the reason `conditions` gives below. Its live on-disk stats
ride `clusterCacheStatsWatch`, which stays current where a field here would freeze at
subscribe time. A cluster owns one per identity it has mirrored; the *active* cache
— the one a sync engine runs for and a UI shows for the row — is the one whose
`spec.serverUid` matches the cluster's last-probed `status.server.uid`, a join the
client derives (it is not a field here, because it depends on the parent's status,
which changes with no cache event and so cannot be kept fresh on this stream).
"""
type ClusterCache {
  "Unique identifier for this cache record (distinct from its parent cluster's id)."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The Cluster this cache belongs to. The client joins caches onto clusters by it."
  clusterID: ClusterID!

  "The record's spec generation, bumped on every spec write."
  generation: Int!
  "When this cache was created — the cluster's identity was first observed."
  createdAt: Time!
  "When this cache's deletion was requested (beehive soft-delete tombstone); null unless deletion is pending. The record lingers until its finalizers clear."
  deletionRequestedAt: Time

  spec: ClusterCacheSpec!
  "The cache-level `Synced` condition: coarse (`Syncing`/`Paused`) — it reports only what this cache decided, not how its sync is doing. Per-component health and freshness live on the cache's per-kind records (`clusterCachedKindsWatch`) or their rollup (`clusterCacheHealthWatch`), which is also why this record carries no status block of its own."
  conditions: [Condition!]!

  """
  The kinds this cache's API server advertises (from `/apis` discovery), read out
  of the cache's own file — the read behind the dashboard's dynamic resource nav.
  Content, not control plane: `cachedKinds` below is the record per kind, this is
  what the store holds. Empty for a cache that has not synced yet. Resolved on
  selection; the live counterpart is `clusterCachedDataKindsWatch`.
  """
  kinds: [ClusterCachedDataKind!]!

  """
  This cache's per-kind sync records, one per kind it mirrors — so hundreds on a
  real cluster — in creation order. The control-plane records, where `kinds` above
  is the mirrored content they describe. Empty until the cache's discovery pass has
  run.

  Resolved on selection; the live counterpart is `clusterCachedKindsWatch(cacheID)`,
  which is what a consumer following per-kind health should read.
  """
  cachedKinds: [ClusterCachedKind!]!

  """
  This cache's control-plane event timeline (newest run first), optionally
  filtered to one `category` (e.g. `"discovery"`, the kind-discovery history) and
  bounded by `limit`. Carries only what the cache itself records — the per-kind sync
  history lives on `ClusterCachedKind.events`. Resolved on selection and
  decoupled from the cache/list watch.
  """
  events(category: String, limit: Int): [Event!]!
}

"""
A cache's desired state. The identity it mirrors, and nothing else: a cache is
created for one physical cluster and never repointed, so there is no field here a
client writes.
"""
type ClusterCacheSpec {
  "The kube-system namespace UID of the physical cluster this cache mirrors — the identity a physical migration turns over. Matches the parent cluster's `status.server.uid` while this is the active cache."
  serverUid: String!
}

"Live on-disk cache statistics for a single cluster."
type ClusterCacheStats {
  "True when a local cache exists on disk."
  exists: Boolean!
  "What the cache costs on disk in bytes — the sqlite file plus its WAL/shm sidecars; 0 when not cached. The headline size: `dbBytes` alone reads low and stale while a sync fills the WAL, since the main file does not grow until a checkpoint lands."
  bytes: Int!
  "The sqlite file alone, in bytes. Part of `bytes`, broken out so a reader can tell a cache holding a lot from one whose WAL is not being checkpointed."
  dbBytes: Int!
  "The write-ahead log, in bytes. Part of `bytes`. Large or growing relative to `dbBytes` means checkpointing is falling behind."
  walBytes: Int!
  "The shared-memory index, in bytes. Part of `bytes`, and present only while the cache is open — a fixed ~32KB, so it is detail rather than news."
  shmBytes: Int!
  "Total number of cached objects across every kind (the sum of the kind catalog's per-kind counts; excludes cached events). 0 when not cached."
  objectCount: Int!
  "Number of distinct kinds with at least one cached object. 0 when not cached."
  kindCount: Int!
  "True when the cache's footprint is over its configured ceiling. Soft by one sweep interval, so a cache can read over before anything acts on it; false for a cache nobody has open, which cannot grow."
  overSizeLimit: Boolean!
  "The ceiling in bytes, 0 when unbounded. Sent so a client can render how close a cache is without hardcoding the sidecar's default."
  sizeLimitBytes: Int!
}

"One frame on the `clusterCachesWatch` stream: a change and the cache it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot."
type ClusterCacheWatchFrame {
  "Whether this cache was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected cache. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; the client removes it by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  cache: ClusterCache
}

"""
One Kubernetes kind being mirrored into a cache — one record per kind the discovery
pass found, so a cache has a hundred or more. Streamed by `clusterCachedKindsWatch`,
which is **cache-scoped** for exactly that reason, and joined onto its cache through
`owner.id`.

This is where per-kind sync health lives. The cache's own `Synced` condition is
coarse — it cannot tell you that one CRD's watch is forbidden while the other
ninety-nine kinds are caught up.
"""
type ClusterCachedKind {
  "Unique identifier for this record."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The ClusterCache this kind is mirrored into — the client's join key."
  owner: ObjectRef!

  "The record's spec generation, bumped on every spec write."
  generation: Int!
  "When this kind was first armed for sync."
  createdAt: Time!
  "When this record's deletion was requested (beehive soft-delete tombstone); null unless deletion is pending. The record lingers until its finalizers clear."
  deletionRequestedAt: Time

  spec: ClusterCachedKindSpec!
  """
  Beehive object conditions. **Empty in practice**: this kind's verdict is a gauge, not a
  stored condition — read it from `clusterCacheSyncStatusWatch`, which carries a row per
  kind, or its cache-wide fold `clusterCacheHealthWatch`. A stored one would serve a dead
  process's answer until the passes caught up.
  """
  conditions: [Condition!]!

  """
  This kind's sync-transition history (newest run first) — the
  `SyncStart`/`SyncComplete`/`SyncDegraded`/`SyncStale`/… runs its worker records,
  optionally filtered to one `category` (e.g. `"sync"`) and bounded by `limit`.
  This is where the per-kind history lives; the parent cache's timeline carries
  only what the cache itself records. Resolved on selection.
  """
  events(category: String, limit: Int): [Event!]!
}

"Which kind a per-kind sync mirrors, and whether the user has left it syncing."
type ClusterCachedKindSpec {
  "Group/version, e.g. \"apps/v1\", or \"v1\" for the core group."
  apiVersion: String!
  "Kind name, e.g. \"Deployment\"."
  kind: String!
  "Plural lowercase resource, e.g. \"deployments\" — the identity the objects watch is opened on."
  resource: String!
  "Whether objects of this kind are namespaced."
  namespaced: Boolean!
  """
  Whether this one kind is syncing, set by `clusterCachedKindSyncEnabledSet`. False stops
  the watch and **keeps the cached rows** — they stay listed and readable, and a resume
  reconciles into them; `clusterCacheClear` is what throws rows away. Independent of the
  cluster-wide `Cluster.spec.syncEnabled`, which freezes every kind at once.
  """
  syncEnabled: Boolean!
}

"One frame on the `clusterCachedKindsWatch` stream: a change and the record it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot."
type ClusterCachedKindWatchFrame {
  "Whether this record was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected record. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; the client removes it by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  kind: ClusterCachedKind
}

"""
One synced kind, identified exactly. `resource` is what a UI renders, but it does not
identify a kind on its own: a CRD may reuse a built-in's plural under another api group,
so anything keying on a kind — looking its sync record up, opening its timeline — needs
the pair.
"""
type SyncedKindRef {
  apiVersion: String!
  resource: String!
}

"""
One cache's sync verdict, folded from every kind it syncs. Shaped like a `Condition` on
purpose — same `status`/`reason`/`message` vocabulary as a per-kind record — so a client
renders a cache exactly as it renders one kind.
"""
type ClusterCacheHealth {
  "The cache this verdict is for. The client keys its map on this and joins onto the cache by it."
  cacheID: ObjectID!
  status: ConditionStatus!
  """
  A cache-level verdict where there is one, and otherwise the dominant per-kind reason.
  Two are cache-level: `Paused` when every kind this cache mirrors has been paused (a
  paused kind is skipped by the fold, so without this arm a fully paused cache would read
  as healthy), and `StoreFailed` when its file will not open, so nothing under it is
  syncing and no kind is in a position to say so. Failing those, the per-kind fold — a hard
  failure beats a stall beats a wait beats a catch-up. `Unknown`/`Syncing` with
  `totalKinds: 0` means the discovery pass has not landed yet — not a fault, and not health
  either.
  """
  reason: String!
  """
  The kinds behind a non-Watching verdict, sorted by plural resource. Empty when healthy.
  A list rather than a rendered phrase: truncation ("+N more") is a layout decision, and a
  client that keys a subscription on the first offender must not have to parse it back out
  of prose — which is also why each entry carries its `apiVersion`, since the plural alone
  does not identify a kind.
  """
  unhealthyKindRefs: [SyncedKindRef!]!
  "How many kinds this cache syncs."
  totalKinds: Int!
  "How many of them are not currently Watching."
  unhealthyKinds: Int!
  """
  How many of `totalKinds` the user has paused. Counted apart rather than subtracted from
  the census: a paused kind is neither unhealthy nor missing, so without this tally it is
  invisible in every reading — a summary spending `unhealthyKinds` alone would report five
  kinds syncing while two of them are switched off.
  """
  pausedKinds: Int!
  """
  The most recent write across every kind — when data last arrived anywhere in this
  cache. Null until some kind has written. On a quiet cluster this legitimately reads
  hours old; `lastLiveAt` is what says that is fine.
  """
  lastUpdateAt: Time
  """
  The OLDEST proof among the kinds that have one — the weakest link, since a cache is
  only as verified as its least-recently proven watch. Null before any kind has proved
  itself.
  """
  lastLiveAt: Time
}

"""
One cache's sync detail: how discovery is doing, and a row per kind it mirrors.

What `ClusterCacheHealth` folds into one verdict for a fleet view, expanded for the one
cache a user opened. Nothing else on the wire carries a per-kind verdict.
"""
type ClusterCacheSyncStatus {
  "The cache this detail is for."
  cacheID: ObjectID!
  "How the kind-discovery sweep is doing. Its own verdict, and not any kind's: a cluster whose `/apis` document will not load has no kinds to report the failure through."
  discovery: ClusterCacheDiscoveryStatus!
  "Every mirrored kind, sorted by `apiVersion` then `resource`, so a rendered list is stable across ticks."
  kinds: [ClusterCacheKindSyncStatus!]!
}

"The kind-discovery sweep's standing answer for one cache."
type ClusterCacheDiscoveryStatus {
  "`Discovering`/`Discovered`/`Partial`/`DiscoveryFailed`, `NoConnection`/`IdentityMismatch` when nothing has reached the server, or `StoreFailed` when the cache's own file will not open. Empty before any sweep has answered."
  reason: String!
  "Human detail behind `reason` — which group-versions did not answer, and why. Empty when there is nothing to add."
  message: String!
}

"One mirrored kind's sync detail. Identity, verdict, freshness, and its row count."
type ClusterCacheKindSyncStatus {
  "The group/version this kind is served at, e.g. `\"apps/v1\"` — or a bare version for the core group."
  apiVersion: String!
  "The singular Kind name, e.g. `\"Deployment\"`."
  kind: String!
  "The lowercase plural URL segment, e.g. `\"deployments\"`. With `apiVersion`, the pair that identifies a kind."
  resource: String!
  "`Syncing`/`Resyncing`/`Resuming`/`Watching`/`Stale`/`SyncFailed`, `NoConnection`/`IdentityMismatch` when the whole cache is waiting on a connection, or `Paused` when this kind's own `spec.syncEnabled` is off. Empty before this kind's worker has answered."
  reason: String!
  "Human detail behind `reason`. Empty when there is nothing to add."
  message: String!
  "When `reason` last moved — \"watching since 10:02\", which is what a live stream has instead of a last-attempt stamp. Null before it has."
  sinceAt: Time
  "When data last arrived for this kind. Null until something has landed; on a quiet collection this legitimately reads hours old, and `lastLiveAt` is what says that is fine."
  lastUpdateAt: Time
  "The last proof the stream is live — a delta OR a bookmark, since bookmarks exist to make an idle watch prove itself. The later of the two stamps, and the only one that tells an idle watch from a wedged one."
  lastLiveAt: Time
  "How many times the stream has come back inside the current healthy stretch — the flapping question a retry countdown cannot answer, since a watch that rotates every thirty seconds never fails."
  restarts: Int!
  "When a kind that is DOWN will be tried again. Null while its stream is up, which is what makes a non-null value the retry streak."
  nextRetryAt: Time
  "How many objects of this kind the cache holds. Read from the store's per-kind counts, so a paused cache still reports what it cached."
  objectCount: Int!
}


# --- Cluster data ---
# What a ClusterCache holds: the discovered kind catalog and the cached Kubernetes
# objects and Events themselves. Every entry point is keyed by
# parent cluster `id` + `cacheID`, and every frame carries its cache provenance so
# a client can reject stragglers from a superseded cache.

"""
One kind a cluster's API server advertises, from `/apis` discovery (built-ins +
CRDs). The dashboard builds its dynamic resource nav from these: `resource` dedupes
against the curated catalog and `apiVersion`'s group buckets the kind into a nav
group.
"""
type ClusterCachedDataKind {
  "Group/version, e.g. \"apps/v1\", or \"v1\" for the core group."
  apiVersion: String!
  "Kind name, e.g. \"Deployment\"."
  kind: String!
  "Plural lowercase resource name (the URL form), e.g. \"deployments\"."
  resource: String!
  "\"Namespaced\" or \"Cluster\"."
  scope: String!
  "True when this kind is backed by a CustomResourceDefinition."
  isCRD: Boolean!
  "The columns a CRD asks a client to render for this version, in declared order; empty for a built-in and for a CRD declaring none. Descriptors only — the server computes no cell values, the client evaluates `jsonPath` against the object's `rawJSON`."
  printerColumns: [PrinterColumn!]!
  "Number of objects of this kind currently in the cache (0 for an advertised-but-empty kind)."
  count: Int!
}

"""
One column from a CRD's `additionalPrinterColumns` — a header and where to read its value
from. Static per kind, so it rides the kinds watch rather than the objects watch.
"""
type PrinterColumn {
  "Column header, e.g. \"Replicas\"."
  name: String!
  "OpenAPI type — integer, number, string, boolean, or date. Drives rendering, not parsing: treat an unrecognized value as a string."
  type: String!
  "Path into the object body, e.g. \".spec.replicas\". A restricted JSONPath; a client that cannot evaluate one renders an empty cell rather than failing the row."
  jsonPath: String!
  "kubectl hides columns above 0 unless `-o wide`."
  priority: Int!
}

"""
One cached Kubernetes Event from a cluster's synced data (the raw `events.k8s.io`/core
Event, read from the active cache). Powers the dashboard's events table. Distinct from
`Event`, which is kstack's own control-plane timeline for a Cluster/ClusterCache — this
is data synced *from* the cluster. The involved-object identity is flattened onto the
record; the raw event body is not exposed.
"""
type ClusterCachedDataEvent {
  "The Event's own object UID — the stable identity the watch keys on."
  uid: String!
  """
  Severity — conventionally `Normal` or `Warning`, but an **open string** (not the
  closed `EventType` enum): Kubernetes does not constrain `Event.type`, so a cluster may
  omit it (empty) or supply a custom value, which is passed through verbatim.
  """
  type: String!
  "CamelCase machine reason, e.g. \"BackOff\" (empty if the Event carried none)."
  reason: String!
  "Human-readable detail (empty if none)."
  message: String!
  "How many times the event has fired (coalesced series count; >= 1)."
  count: Int!
  "First occurrence time, or null when the source Event carried no timestamp."
  firstSeen: Time
  "Latest occurrence time, or null when the source Event carried no timestamp."
  lastSeen: Time
  "Kind of the object the event is about, e.g. \"Pod\" (empty if unset)."
  involvedKind: String!
  "Namespace of the involved object (empty for a cluster-scoped or name-only reference)."
  involvedNamespace: String!
  "Name of the involved object (empty if unset)."
  involvedName: String!
}

"""
One cached Kubernetes object projected for a table row (any kind), read from the
active cache. Only the universal identity is typed for now (`uid`/`apiVersion`/`kind`/
`namespace`/`name`/`creationTimestamp`) — enough to key the watch, sort, and render a
Name/Namespace/Age table. The nested object body (and kind-specific columns computed
from it) is deferred to a follow-up: see the "ClusterCachedDataObject — native nested body"
item in `docs/TODO.md`. Distinct from `ClusterCachedDataEvent`, which is one specific kind with its
own fully-typed shape.
"""
type ClusterCachedDataObject {
  "The object's UID — the stable identity the watch keys on."
  uid: String!
  "The object's group/version, e.g. \"apps/v1\", or \"v1\" for the core group."
  apiVersion: String!
  "Kind name, e.g. \"Deployment\"."
  kind: String!
  "Namespace; empty for a cluster-scoped kind."
  namespace: String!
  "Object name."
  name: String!
  "creationTimestamp — drives the universal Age column; null when the source object carried none."
  creationTimestamp: Time
  "Full native body as JSON, verbatim from the cache (managedFields and the kubectl last-applied annotation stripped). Read only for the rows that become frames, so a watch pays for what it sends rather than for the collection; null on a `Deleted` frame's object, and when the stored body would not load."
  rawJSON: JSON
}

"One frame on the `clusterCachedDataKindsWatch` stream: a change and the kind it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot. Either way it carries its cache provenance."
type ClusterCachedDataKindWatchFrame {
  "Whether this kind was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified (chiefly a `count` change), or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected kind. On `Deleted`, its last-known row; the client keys on `apiVersion` + `resource`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  kind: ClusterCachedDataKind
  """
  The cache this frame came from — the frame's provenance. A client watching the
  active cache rejects a late frame from a superseded cache (one still draining
  after a cache/context switch) by comparing this against the cache it subscribed for.
  """
  cacheID: ObjectID!
}

"One frame on the `clusterCachedDataEventsWatch` stream: a change and the event it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot. Either way it carries its cache provenance."
type ClusterCachedDataEventWatchFrame {
  "Whether this event was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified (chiefly a `count`/`lastSeen` change as it re-fires), or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected event. On `Deleted`, its last-known row; the client keys on `uid`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  event: ClusterCachedDataEvent
  """
  The cache this frame came from — the frame's provenance, mirroring
  `ClusterCachedDataKindWatchFrame.cacheID`. A client watching the active cache rejects a late
  frame from a superseded cache by comparing this against the cache it subscribed for.
  """
  cacheID: ObjectID!
}

"One frame on the `clusterCachedDataObjectsWatch` stream: a change and the object it affected, or the `Bookmark` closing the snapshot. Either way it carries its (cache, kind) provenance."
type ClusterCachedDataObjectWatchFrame {
  "Whether this object was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected object. On `Deleted`, its last-known identity with a null `rawJSON` — the client keys the removal on `uid` and never reads the body. Null on `Bookmark`."
  object: ClusterCachedDataObject
  """
  The cache this frame came from — the frame's provenance, mirroring
  `ClusterCachedDataKindWatchFrame.cacheID`. A client watching the active cache rejects a late
  frame from a superseded cache by comparing this against the cache it subscribed for.
  """
  cacheID: ObjectID!
  """
  The kind this frame is for — the watch's `(apiVersion, resource)`. Unlike the kinds/
  events watches (whose variables carry no kind, so `cacheID` is complete provenance),
  this watch is keyed by kind too, so a client switching resources *within one cache*
  uses these to reject a straggler from the previous kind's still-draining subscription.
  """
  apiVersion: String!
  resource: String!
}

# --- Models ---
# What the sidecar can answer with. The catalog is whatever had a key at startup; a
# feature that calls a model names one of these per call. A provider is named by its
# id, a string rather than an enum: a provider is data, and one added at runtime has
# no member.

"""
The providers whose transcripts replay on one another, named for the API that defined the
shape.
"""
enum LLMDialect {
  "Anthropic's Messages API."
  Messages
  "OpenAI's Responses API."
  Responses
  "OpenAI Chat Completions: every local runtime's wire, and most cloud vendors'."
  ChatCompletions
  "The fake adapter of a debug build."
  Fake
}

"A provider the sidecar can send a turn to."
type Provider {
  "Stable for the life of the app: what `chatSend` and a stored message name."
  id: String!
  "What a picker shows as the group heading."
  label: String!
  "The dialect this provider speaks. Null on a provider the sidecar no longer holds — a stored message's, labelled by its id — since nothing but the id is known of it."
  llmDialect: LLMDialect
}

"A model the sidecar can answer with."
type Model {
  provider: Provider!
  id: String!
  label: String!
  "The effort levels this model accepts, in the provider's own words, least to most. Empty when it has no such knob."
  efforts: [String!]!
  "The level a picker starts on. Empty when `efforts` is."
  defaultEffort: String!
}

# --- Chat ---
# The app's own conversations, held in app.db. Sending a message is a save, not a
# phone call: chatSend returns once the turn is accepted and the answer arrives on
# chatMessagesWatch. See docs/adr/2026-09-10-the-answer-is-a-record.md.

"""
The identity of a chat (a UUID string on the wire). Distinct from `ObjectID`: chats
are not beehive objects and share none of that id machinery. Identity alone: order
a list by `updatedAt` and a transcript by `seq`. Use values returned by the server
verbatim; do not construct or parse them.
"""
scalar ChatID

"The identity of one message (a UUID string on the wire). Never a `ChatID`."
scalar MessageID

"""
Who produced a message, matching the Messages API wire vocabulary. Not a display hint:
a `User` message carrying only tool results is machinery, not something a person typed.
What to draw comes from the content blocks.
"""
enum ChatMessageRole {
  User
  Assistant
}

"How far along an assistant's turn is. A user message is always `Complete`."
enum ChatMessageStatus {
  "Still being written. Reconciled to `Failed` if the sidecar restarts."
  Streaming
  "Stopped on a command the user has not decided: in flight, drawn with the approval request."
  WaitingApproval
  Complete
  "Ended badly; `error` says how. Never retried automatically."
  Failed
  "Stopped by `chatCancel` or by shutdown. The partial text is kept."
  Cancelled
}

"""
Which of the app's two modes a chat belongs to. Set when the chat is created and never
changed. Each mode lists only its own chats.
"""
enum ChatMode {
  Chat
  Dashboard
}

type Chat {
  id: ChatID!
  "Taken from the first user message, in the transaction that creates the chat. Renameable."
  title: String!
  "Where this chat is listed. Fixed at creation."
  mode: ChatMode!
  "The cluster this chat was started under. Fixed at creation. Each cluster lists only its own chats."
  clusterID: ClusterID!
  createdAt: Time!
  "Last activity: a message created or settled, or a rename. What the recent list sorts by."
  updatedAt: Time!
  "Whether any run of the chat waits on the user: its answers' own, or an agent's under one of them."
  awaitingApproval: Boolean!
  "Whether the user disabled the sandbox for this chat: its commands run outside it, each asking first."
  sandboxDisabled: Boolean!
}

"Whether sandboxed Bash is offered on this machine. It is fixed for the sidecar's life, so this is a query and not a watch."
type SandboxStatus {
  available: Boolean!
  "What the probe found, such as Seatbelt or bwrap at /usr/bin/bwrap, or why there is none, in the sidecar's words."
  reason: String!
}

"""
One message. An assistant message is also the record of its turn: while `status` is
`Streaming` its `content` grows, re-sent whole on every frame — so any one frame is
disposable.
"""
type ChatMessage {
  id: MessageID!
  "The chat this belongs to — the client's join key."
  chatID: ChatID!
  "Position in the chat. Order by this, never by `createdAt`."
  seq: Int!
  role: ChatMessageRole!
  """
  The content blocks as they went over the wire. Partial while `Streaming`. A reader may find
  `text` (what a person reads), `thinking` (the summary of an answer's thinking), `native` (a
  provider's own block, kept for it alone), `context` (the cluster card the app attached to a
  question), `tool_use` (the model asking for a tool: `id`, `name`, `input`), `tool_result`
  (the sidecar's answer to one, by `id`, with `isError` when it refused) and `server_use` (a
  call the provider ran, in a `tool_use`'s fields; its row is in `toolCalls`). Each has a
  `type`; `text`, `thinking`, `context` and `tool_result` carry their `text`.
  """
  content: JSON!
  "The summary of the model's thinking, read off the blocks whatever shape holds it. Empty where none was kept."
  thinking: String!
  status: ChatMessageStatus!
  """
  Whether a run of the answer waits on the user: its own, or an agent's under one of its `Agent`
  calls. An agent can wait under an answer that has settled, so this, not `status`, says whether a
  request is live.
  """
  awaitingApproval: Boolean!
  "Why the turn failed; empty otherwise. Never a raw provider error."
  error: String!

  "The model this send asked for — the catalog id it named, not what the provider answered as. Empty on a user message."
  model: String!
  """
  The provider whose model it was. Null on a user message, which no provider wrote. A
  message outlives its provider: one the sidecar no longer holds is labelled by its id.
  """
  provider: Provider
  "How hard it was asked to think, in the provider's own words. Empty where the model has no such knob, and on a user message."
  effort: String!
  "The last model call's."
  finishReason: String!

  createdAt: Time!
  "When the turn settled, whatever its status; null while it runs and on a user message, which has no turn."
  finishedAt: Time
  "The turn's tool calls, off their rows, in the order the model asked. Empty on a user message."
  toolCalls: [ToolCall!]!
  "What the answer's text cites, in order, read by its run's dialect. Empty where it cites nothing."
  citations: [Citation!]!
}

"One citation an answer's text makes. Every field is text the provider sent: read as data, drawn as text."
type Citation {
  "The provider's word for the kind: `web_search_result_location`, `page_location`, …"
  type: String!
  "The source's address. Empty for a source with none, a document the model was given."
  url: String!
  "The page's or the document's title. Empty where it had none."
  title: String!
  "The passage the text rests on."
  citedText: String!
}

"An approvals row's id. Opaque text, like every other id."
scalar ApprovalID

"A tool call row's id. Opaque text, like every other id."
scalar ToolCallID

"One call of a tool in an answer's turn, off its `tool_calls` row."
type ToolCall {
  id: ToolCallID!
  "The provider's id of the block it answers: a `tool_use`, or a provider call's `server_use`."
  toolUseID: String!
  "The tool's name in the box: a tool of ours by the name it is offered under, a vendor's by its own name."
  name: String!
  "The vendor's own identifier for the shape the call uses (`web_search_20260318`). Empty for a tool of ours."
  contract: String!
  "The call's input, as the model sent it."
  arguments: JSON!
  "What kind of thing the call did, whoever ran it. Null for a call of a tool this build does not know."
  actionKind: ToolActionKind
  "Where the call is. Null on a `Provider` call: the app ran nothing, so it has no status to report."
  status: ToolCallStatus
  "Who ran it. A `Provider` call ran on the model's provider: never asked about, and with no output of its own."
  runsOn: ToolCallRunsOn!
  "What the call does, read from its arguments by its own tool. Null when the tool has no way to show a call, or refuses the arguments."
  action: ToolAction
  "The user's decision on the call; null on a call no one is asked about. Decide it while the call is `AwaitingApproval`."
  approval: ToolCallApproval
  "What the model read: the tool's text, or the refusal object. Empty until the call is over."
  output: String!
  isError: Boolean!
  "The background task the call started; null on every call that started none."
  background: BackgroundTask
  "The `Agent` call a subagent's call ran under; null on the answer's own calls."
  agentCallID: ToolCallID
  "Every request the call's sandboxed command sent to change the cluster, in the order asked. Empty on every call but a sandboxed command's."
  clusterWrites: [ClusterWrite!]!
}

"""
A request a sandboxed command sent to change the cluster, held for the user. It waits while its
approval is `Pending` and its call `Running`.
"""
type ClusterWrite {
  approval: ToolCallApproval!
  "The HTTP method: POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE."
  method: String!
  "The path and query, exactly as sent."
  path: String!
  "The path's subresource, as the proxy parsed it; empty for none."
  subresource: String!
  "The media type, while the write waits; empty otherwise."
  contentType: String!
  "The body, exactly as sent, while the write waits; empty otherwise, and for a DELETE with none. The record keeps it."
  body: String!
  "Whether the request asks for a dry run: a POST, PUT or PATCH whose every `dryRun` is `All`. Never a DELETE."
  dryRun: Boolean!
}

"What a call does, from the tool that made it. Never read from the call's arguments."
enum ToolActionKind {
  Command
  Read
  Write
  Edit
  Search
  Fetch
  Stop
  Memory
  Delegate
  KubeQuery
}

"A command started with `run_in_background`, or an agent an `Agent` call launched, off its `background_tasks` row."
type BackgroundTask {
  status: BackgroundTaskStatus!
  "Set on an `Exited` task whose code could be read."
  exitCode: Int
  "A `Completed` agent's report, cut to the inline limit. Empty for a command, and until the agent completes."
  report: String!
}

"""
Where a background task is. A command ends `Exited`; an agent `Completed` (it answered) or
`Failed` (it could not). `Stopped` was ended by the model, the user, the app's quit, or an agent's
request that went unanswered; `Lost` was running when the sidecar stopped unexpectedly, and a
command may still be running.
"""
enum BackgroundTaskStatus {
  Running
  Exited
  Completed
  Failed
  Stopped
  Lost
}

"What a call does: the model's description, and exactly one kind."
type ToolAction {
  "The model's own account of the call. Empty when it gave none."
  description: String!
  command: CommandAction
  read: ReadAction
  write: WriteAction
  edit: EditAction
  search: SearchAction
  fetch: FetchAction
  memory: MemoryAction
  delegate: DelegateAction
  kubeQuery: KubeQueryAction
}

"A statement over the chat's cluster's cache. The model wrote it, so draw it as text."
type KubeQueryAction {
  "The statement as it runs, without a trailing semicolon."
  sql: String!
  "The most rows the call asked for: 200 unless it named a number, at most 2000."
  limit: Int!
}

"A task handed to a subagent. Every field is the parent model's text."
type DelegateAction {
  "The brief the parent wrote: the subagent's whole task."
  prompt: String!
  "The kind of agent: `general-purpose`."
  agentType: String!
  "The model the subagent runs on, by its id. Empty when it runs on the parent's."
  model: String!
}

"A web search: what left the machine."
type SearchAction {
  "The query. Empty for a call that sent none."
  query: String!
}

"A page fetched: the URL as the sidecar fetches it, and the host it dials."
type FetchAction {
  "The URL, upgraded to https, its host in ASCII."
  url: String!
  "The host, in ASCII, with the port when it is not 443."
  host: String!
}

"""
A call of the Memory tool as its arguments name it. The model may have written it
from cluster data, so draw every field as text.
"""
type MemoryAction {
  "`save` or `forget`."
  op: String!
  name: String!
  "Empty on a forget."
  body: String!
  "`cluster` or `everywhere`: who the note reaches. `cluster` when the call names none."
  scope: String!
}

"Who ran a tool call: the sidecar, on the user's machine, or the model's provider, on its own servers."
enum ToolCallRunsOn {
  Sidecar
  Provider
}

"A command, exactly as the shell receives it."
type CommandAction {
  "What runs, byte for byte."
  text: String!
  "Where it started, exactly as the shell starts there. Empty when the call never reached the gate."
  cwd: String!
  "Whether it keeps running after its call answers, until it exits or is stopped."
  background: Boolean!
  "Whether a sandbox confined the command."
  sandboxed: Boolean!
}

"A read of a file, as the call named it."
type ReadAction {
  path: String!
}

"A file written whole, as the call named it."
type WriteAction {
  path: String!
  "The file's new content, byte for byte."
  content: String!
}

"A replacement in a file, as the call named it."
type EditAction {
  path: String!
  "The text that goes, byte for byte."
  oldString: String!
  "The text that replaces it, byte for byte."
  newString: String!
  "Every occurrence, not one."
  replaceAll: Boolean!
}

"The user's decision on a gated call, off its `approvals` row."
type ToolCallApproval {
  id: ApprovalID!
  status: ApprovalStatus!
}

"""
Where the user's decision is. `Pending` until it commits, and after, when the call's turn ended
before anyone answered: the record of a question nobody answered. An `Approved` call whose status
is `NotRun` was approved and never started. `Abandoned` is a cluster write whose wait ended with no
decision while its call ran on; a call's own approval never takes it.
"""
enum ApprovalStatus {
  Pending
  Approved
  Denied
  Abandoned
}

"""
Where a tool call is. `NotRun` never started; `Interrupted` started and was cut off with no
answer from its tool (a cancel, the loop's deadline, a crash), so it may have run; `Failed` is a
tool's own error.
"""
enum ToolCallStatus {
  NotRun
  AwaitingApproval
  Denied
  Running
  Succeeded
  Failed
  Interrupted
}

type ChatWatchFrame {
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected chat. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; remove by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  chat: Chat
}

type ChatMessageWatchFrame {
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected message. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; remove by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  message: ChatMessage
}

# --- Memory ---

"""
The identity of a memory (a UUID string on the wire). Like `ChatID`, not an
`ObjectID`: a memory is a row, not a beehive object. Use values returned by the
server verbatim.
"""
scalar MemoryID

"Who last wrote a memory."
enum MemoryAuthor {
  Model
  User
}

"""
A note kept for later chats, one fact each: for one cluster, or for every cluster.
The model may have written it from cluster data, so it is text to draw as text.
"""
type Memory {
  id: MemoryID!
  "The cluster it is for. Null is every cluster."
  clusterID: ClusterID
  name: String!
  body: String!
  writtenBy: MemoryAuthor!
  updatedAt: Time!
}

type MemoryWatchFrame {
  type: DeltaFrameType!
  "The affected memory. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; remove by `id`. Null on `Bookmark`."
  memory: Memory
}

input MemorySaveInput {
  "The memory to rewrite. Null creates one."
  id: MemoryID
  "The cluster it is for. Null is every cluster."
  clusterID: ClusterID
  name: String!
  body: String!
}

# --- Cloud account ---
# The sidecar-owned sign-in and display identity. Tokens never appear here.
# Login verifies claims; startup restores display claims without re-verification.

"Display identity from OIDC ID-token claims. Verified at login, restored without verification at startup; never an authorization input."
type Identity {
  sub: String!
  email: String!
  name: String!
}

"Local session state. `authenticated` means a refresh token is stored, not that the server has just validated it. `identity` is display-only and non-null only when authenticated."
type AuthState {
  authenticated: Boolean!
  identity: Identity
}

"What the sandbox does with one folder of the user's PATH."
enum SandboxPathState {
  "On the sandbox's PATH and readable in it."
  Adopted
  "Listed by the shell and waiting for the user: adopting it would open more to commands, or its group can add programs to it."
  Pending
  "Removed by the user, and kept so a launch does not adopt it again."
  Gone
}

"Whose decision a PATH entry's state is."
enum SandboxPathSource {
  "Kstack's, when it read the login shell's PATH."
  Shell
  "The user's, by Include or Remove."
  User
}

"One folder of the user's PATH, and what the sandbox does with it."
type SandboxPathEntry {
  "The folder as the shell gave it."
  dir: String!
  "The folder dir resolved to when its state was set; the one the sandbox reads."
  target: String!
  state: SandboxPathState!
  source: SandboxPathSource!
  "Whether target was writable by a group other than an administrators' one, so others in it can add programs to it."
  shared: Boolean!
}

"One value the security settings file held and the sidecar left out."
type SecurityRefusal {
  "The setting's JSON key in the file."
  field: String!
  "What the setting's section names the value by, else its JSON."
  value: String!
  "Why it was left out, in the user's words."
  reason: String!
}

# --- Root operations ---
# Every root field, grouped by the noun it belongs to.

type Query {
  # --- Model ---
  "Every model the sidecar can answer with, in catalog order — the first entry is what a picker starts on. Fixed for the process's life, so this is a query and not a watch."
  models: [Model!]!

  # --- Sandbox ---
  "Whether this machine offers sandboxed Bash, and why."
  sandbox: SandboxStatus!

  # --- Cluster ---
  "A single cluster by id; null if not tracked."
  cluster(id: ClusterID!): Cluster

  "Every tracked cluster — those in the kubeconfig plus any with a leftover cache. Sorted by `spec.name`, falling back to the kube-context for a cluster the user has not named."
  clusters: [Cluster!]!

  # --- ClusterCache ---
  "A single cache by its own id, not its cluster's; null if unknown."
  clusterCache(id: ObjectID!): ClusterCache

  "Every cache, or one cluster's when `clusterID` is given — the set `Cluster.caches` serves."
  clusterCaches(clusterID: ClusterID): [ClusterCache!]!

  "A single synced-kind record by its own id, not its cache's; null if unknown."
  clusterCachedKind(id: ObjectID!): ClusterCachedKind

  "Every per-kind sync record — hundreds unscoped — or one cache's when `cacheID` is given; the set `ClusterCache.cachedKinds` serves."
  clusterCachedKinds(cacheID: ObjectID): [ClusterCachedKind!]!

  # --- Sandbox ---
  "Every value the security settings file held that the sidecar left out at start, each with its reason. Fixed for the process's life, so this is a query and not a watch."
  securityRefused: [SecurityRefusal!]!

  "The frozen PATH sandboxed commands search, in the shell's order. Empty on a machine with no sandbox."
  sandboxPath: [SandboxPathEntry!]!

  "Why the last read of the login shell's PATH failed, at launch or on refresh; null once one answers, and on a machine with no sandbox."
  sandboxPathFault: String

  "Whether a sync has ever read the login shell's PATH. Until one has, sandboxed commands search the platform's default folders; after it, the list alone, so an empty one searches nothing. False on a machine with no sandbox."
  sandboxPathResolved: Boolean!

  # --- Cloud account ---
  "Current authentication state. Signed-out when no cloud account is configured; live updates via authStateWatch."
  authState: AuthState!
}

type Mutation {
  # --- Cluster ---
  "Enable or disable a cluster in the app. Disabling keeps the record tracked but dormant (no connection, hidden from context pickers). Returns the updated cluster."
  clusterEnabledSet(id: ClusterID!, enabled: Boolean!): Cluster!

  "Enable or disable syncing for a cluster. Disabling stops sync and freezes the cache. Returns the updated cluster."
  clusterSyncEnabledSet(id: ClusterID!, syncEnabled: Boolean!): Cluster!

  "Let the background monitor run against this cluster, or stop it. Returns the updated record."
  clusterMonitoringEnabledSet(id: ClusterID!, monitoringEnabled: Boolean!): Cluster!

  "Force an immediate reconnection attempt, resetting any backoff. Resolves once the probe it asked for has finished — not when it was scheduled — so a caller can show the retry as busy for exactly that long. What the probe found arrives on the cluster's conditions. Returns true."
  clusterConnectionRetry(id: ClusterID!): Boolean!

  "Delete an orphaned cluster. Returns true as soon as the deletion is requested: the record then carries `deletionRequestedAt` while its runtime, its caches and its chats are torn down, and leaves `clustersWatch` once they are gone. Refused for a cluster its source still declares — the discovery pass would re-import it under a fresh id, losing the user's toggles; stop tracking it at the source, or disable it. Deleting a record already gone, or already deleting, is not an error."
  clusterDelete(id: ClusterID!): Boolean!

  # --- ClusterCache ---
  "Clear one cache's on-disk file but keep its record. Its sync workers are restarted onto the emptied file, so a present, enabled cluster re-syncs from scratch. Takes the cache's own id, not its cluster's: a UID migration leaves a cluster owning more than one. Returns the cleared cache."
  clusterCacheClear(id: ObjectID!): ClusterCache!

  # --- ClusterCachedKind ---
  "Stop or resume syncing ONE kind, leaving the rest of the cache alone. Pausing keeps the cached rows — they stay listed and readable throughout, and a resume reconciles into them rather than starting from nothing (off the watch cookie if the pause was short enough for the server to still serve it, otherwise by relisting). A discovery sweep will not undo it: the sweep owns the kind's identity, the user owns this switch. Returns the updated record."
  clusterCachedKindSyncEnabledSet(id: ObjectID!, syncEnabled: Boolean!): ClusterCachedKind!

  # --- Chat ---
  "Save a user message and start the assistant's turn. Returns the assistant's message as accepted, not as finished — the answer arrives on chatMessagesWatch. A null `chatID` creates the chat; a chat already running a turn is refused. `requestID` makes the send idempotent for as long as its chat lives, and a repeat is answered by that id alone: this call's `chatID`, `model`, `effort` and `content` are ignored. The key goes with the chat, so a repeat after its delete is a new send. A null `chatID` creates the chat in `mode` and under `clusterID`, which must name an existing cluster; otherwise both are ignored, and on a `requestID` replay they are ignored like `chatID` and `content`. `sandboxDisabled` is the chat's switch as the sender saw it, `false` for a chat the send creates; a send whose switch differs from the chat's is refused with `KSTACK_CHAT_SANDBOX_CHANGED`, and a replay ignores it too."
  chatSend(chatID: ChatID, mode: ChatMode!, clusterID: ClusterID!, sandboxDisabled: Boolean!, providerID: String!, modelID: String!, effort: String!, requestID: String!, content: String!): ChatMessage!

  "Stop the in-flight turn, keeping the partial answer. Returns true; a no-op when nothing is running."
  chatCancel(chatID: ChatID!): Boolean!

  "Decide a waiting command. Returns true when a turn was waiting on it; that says the decision reached the turn, not that it was recorded, since a cancel landing in the same moment can still win and leave the approval pending. A decision nothing waits on — the turn cancelled, stranded or already decided — returns false and changes nothing."
  approvalDecide(id: ApprovalID!, approve: Boolean!): Boolean!

  "The user's stop of the background task a call started: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after a grace. Returns true when a task of that call was running; false, changing nothing, when none is."
  backgroundTaskStop(id: ToolCallID!): Boolean!

  "Retitle a chat. Returns the record it committed, whose `updatedAt` the rename moves."
  chatRename(id: ChatID!, title: String!): Chat!

  "Switch a chat to run its commands outside the sandbox, each asking first, or back in it. Takes effect from the chat's next turn; what already started keeps running as it started. Leaves `updatedAt` alone. Refused with `KSTACK_VALIDATION_ERROR` on a machine with no sandbox. Returns the record it committed."
  chatSandboxDisabledSet(id: ChatID!, sandboxDisabled: Boolean!): Chat!

  "Delete a chat and its messages. Instant and permanent — every window watching it sees the rows go. Deleting one already gone is not an error. Returns true."
  chatDelete(id: ChatID!): Boolean!

  # --- Sandbox ---
  "Include a pending or removed PATH entry, for `target`, the folder the user was shown. Refused KSTACK_VALIDATION_ERROR for a dir not listed or already included, one whose target has moved since, while the settings file holds an entry Kstack cannot read, or on a machine with no sandbox. Returns the whole list."
  sandboxPathInclude(dir: String!, target: String!): [SandboxPathEntry!]!

  "Remove a PATH entry. Refused KSTACK_VALIDATION_ERROR for a dir not listed or already removed, or on a machine with no sandbox. Returns the whole list."
  sandboxPathRemove(dir: String!): [SandboxPathEntry!]!

  "Run the login shell again and fold its PATH in. Refused KSTACK_VALIDATION_ERROR with the shell's fault, or on a machine with no sandbox. Returns the whole list."
  sandboxPathRefresh: [SandboxPathEntry!]!

  # --- Memory ---
  "Create a memory, or rewrite one by `id`; a rewrite can rename it and move it between one cluster and every cluster. Written by the user. Refused `KSTACK_MEMORY_NAME_TAKEN` for a name the target scope holds, `KSTACK_MEMORY_FULL` for a scope with no room for the memory, `KSTACK_MEMORY_SECRET` for a credential, `KSTACK_VALIDATION_ERROR` for a note out of shape, and `KSTACK_RECORD_NOT_FOUND` for an unknown id or a missing cluster."
  memorySave(input: MemorySaveInput!): Memory!

  "Delete a memory. Refused `KSTACK_RECORD_NOT_FOUND` for an unknown id. Returns true."
  memoryDelete(id: MemoryID!): Boolean!

  # --- Cloud account ---
  "Start the sidecar-owned browser sign-in (OAuth). Setup (loopback bind + browser open) runs synchronously, so a setup failure — or no cloud account — returns an error. Returns true once the browser opens; the token exchange then completes in the background, with the signed-in state delivered via authStateWatch."
  authLoginStart: Boolean!

  "Sign out: clear local credentials, broadcast signed-out, and revoke the token server-side (best-effort). Returns true; no-op without a cloud account."
  authLogout: Boolean!
}

type Subscription {
  # Two live shapes, named in every description below. A DELTA WATCH replays the current
  # set, closes that snapshot with one Bookmark, then streams changes (DeltaFrameType) —
  # so a consumer renders an empty state only after the Bookmark. A GAUGE is
  # current-on-subscribe: its first value IS the snapshot, and no Bookmark rides it.
  # Every subscription here is endless.

  # --- Any object ---
  "A cache's or a per-kind record's event timeline — `ClusterCache`, `ClusterCachedKind` — as an event log: runs upserted by `Event.id`, the snapshot closed by one `Bookmark` (`EventFrameType`). Optionally one `category`. The point read is that record's own `events` field; a cluster's timeline is `clusterEventsWatch`."
  eventsWatch(id: ObjectID!, category: String): EventWatchFrame!

  # --- Cluster ---
  "The cluster list as a delta watch, keyed by `cluster.id`. Cache sync status is not here — it streams on `clusterCachesWatch`."
  clustersWatch: ClusterWatchFrame!

  "A cluster's event timeline as an event log, the same shape as `eventsWatch`. Bookmark-only for an id naming nothing."
  clusterEventsWatch(id: ClusterID!, category: String): EventWatchFrame!

  "A cluster's next reconcile as a gauge — what keeps a disconnected cluster's countdown live, since scheduling fires no object watch."
  clusterScheduleWatch(id: ClusterID!): Schedule!

  # --- ClusterCache ---
  "Cache records as a delta watch, keyed by `cache.id` and joined onto clusters by `clusterID`. Carries no ordering guarantee against `clustersWatch`."
  clusterCachesWatch: ClusterCacheWatchFrame!

  "Every cache's folded sync verdict as a gauge, keyed by `cacheID`. No deletes — a verdict's lifetime is its cache's, so the client drops one when its cache leaves `clusterCachesWatch`."
  clusterCacheHealthWatch: ClusterCacheHealth!

  "One cache's per-kind sync records as a delta watch, keyed by `kind.id`. Cache-scoped, since there is one record per synced kind."
  clusterCachedKindsWatch(cacheID: ObjectID!): ClusterCachedKindWatchFrame!

  "One cache's on-disk contents as a gauge. A subscription, not a `ClusterCache` field, which would freeze at subscribe time."
  clusterCacheStatsWatch(id: ClusterID!, cacheID: ObjectID!): ClusterCacheStats!

  "One cache's sync detail as a gauge — the discovery verdict and a row per mirrored kind. Re-read on a cadence rather than on a signal: its counts and freshness stamps move while every record under it sits still. Holds silent for a pair naming no live cache."
  clusterCacheSyncStatusWatch(id: ClusterID!, cacheID: ObjectID!): ClusterCacheSyncStatus!

  # --- Cluster data ---
  "One cache's discovered kind catalog as a delta watch, keyed by `apiVersion` + `resource`. Counts are live, so an object write re-emits its kind. The live counterpart of `ClusterCache.kinds`."
  clusterCachedDataKindsWatch(
    id: ClusterID!
    cacheID: ObjectID!
  ): ClusterCachedDataKindWatchFrame!

  "One cache's cached Kubernetes Events as a delta watch, newest first, keyed by `uid`; an event the server dropped surfaces as `Deleted`. Distinct from `eventsWatch`, which streams kstack's own timeline."
  clusterCachedDataEventsWatch(
    id: ClusterID!
    cacheID: ObjectID!
  ): ClusterCachedDataEventWatchFrame!

  "One kind's cached objects from a cache as a delta watch, keyed by `uid`. Scoped by `apiVersion` + `resource`, e.g. `\"apps/v1\"` + `\"deployments\"`."
  clusterCachedDataObjectsWatch(
    id: ClusterID!
    cacheID: ObjectID!
    apiVersion: String!
    resource: String!
  ): ClusterCachedDataObjectWatchFrame!

  # --- Chat ---
  "DELTA WATCH. The chat list, keyed by `chat.id` — what the sidebar renders."
  chatsWatch: ChatWatchFrame!

  """
  DELTA WATCH. One chat's messages, keyed by `message.id`. A streaming answer arrives
  as repeated `Modified` frames carrying the whole text so far, so any one frame is
  disposable. Nothing orders this against chatsWatch: on a delete, the chat's `Deleted`
  and its messages' come from two pumps and land in either order.
  """
  chatMessagesWatch(chatID: ChatID!): ChatMessageWatchFrame!

  # --- Memory ---
  "DELTA WATCH. What one cluster's chats see, keyed by `memory.id`: its own memories and every cluster's. A move between scopes arrives as `Added` or `Deleted` on each cluster it enters or leaves."
  memoriesWatch(clusterID: ClusterID!): MemoryWatchFrame!

  # --- Cloud account ---
  "Authentication state as a gauge: a fresh snapshot on every sign-in, sign-out, and refresh failure."
  authStateWatch: AuthState!
}
